# Culvert Maintenance Agent — sudoers allowlist
#
# This file is the SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for which `docker compose`
# invocations the agent is allowed to run when privilege_mode=sudoers
# (the production default; see roadmap/D1.6-maintenance-agent-
# implementation-plan.md § 4.5).
#
# The agent's command runner prepends `sudo -n` to every templated
# invocation; sudo's exact-arg matching enforces that the agent cannot
# deviate from this list, even with an in-process bug.
#
# Adding a new operation requires the four-step contract from § 4.6:
#   1. Typed API handler in the agent.
#   2. Command template in internal/runner.
#   3. Sudoers allowlist entries HERE (this file).
#   4. Tests, including the parity test that asserts every runner
#      template has matching lines and vice versa.
#
# Skipping any of the four is a review block.
#
# D1.6a scope: minimal `docker compose ps` entry for /v1/status.
# D1.6b scope: backup (encrypted/unencrypted/list), restore
#   (dryrun/commit), compose down, compose up -d, cleanup leftovers
#   (dryrun/commit). Every variable-argument cli operation goes through
#   the root-owned compose-cli wrapper (see below).
# Upgrade check scope: read-only image inspect + manifest inspect for
#   the upgrade-availability check (direct rules, sole trailing `*`).
# Upgrade apply / rollback scope: running-container inspect, digest-pinned
#   pull and retag to culvert/proxy:pinned, all through the compose-cli
#   wrapper (see the section at the end of this file).
#
# Operator install steps:
#   The recommended path is to run packaging/culvert-maint/install.sh,
#   which reads compose_project_dir and compose_file from
#   /etc/culvert-maint/config.toml, renders the sudoers file with the
#   *full* compose path, runs `visudo -c` to validate, and refuses to
#   complete the install if any placeholder remains.
#
#   For manual installation, replace {compose_path} below with the
#   absolute, joined path:
#       <compose_project_dir>/<compose_file>
#   Then validate with:
#       visudo -c -f /etc/sudoers.d/culvert-maint
#   And install with mode 0440 root:root:
#       install -m 0440 -o root -g root \
#           packaging/sudoers/culvert-maint \
#           /etc/sudoers.d/culvert-maint
#
# IMPORTANT: this file MUST be path-bound. sudo's exact-arg matching
# does NOT honor the agent's process cwd; without the absolute path
# the allowlist would accept the same `docker compose -f X ps` from
# any working directory the agent could be coerced into.
#
# NO wildcard inside a command argument. sudo-rs (the default sudo on
# Ubuntu 26.04) rejects any `*` or `?` in a command argument ("wildcards
# are not allowed in command arguments"); the only argument wildcard it
# still accepts is a SOLE trailing `*`, meaning "any further arguments".
# The static test TestSudoers_NoWildcardInCommandArguments
# (cmd/culvert-maint/internal/runner/parity_test.go) enforces that rule so
# a regression fails on any runner image, not only on a sudo-rs host.
#
# The same file must also carry NO glob character class (`[0-9a-f]`) and
# NO backslash-escaped space in an argument: sudo-rs parses both but compares
# arguments literally, so such a rule silently never matches (the same test
# flags them).
#
# The compose `cli` operations whose arguments vary per call (backup
# encrypted/unencrypted, restore dryrun/commit, cleanup dryrun/commit) and the
# upgrade path's container inspect, pinned pull and retag are therefore NOT
# allowlisted as `docker compose … /backup/*` or `[0-9a-f]` patterns any
# more. They are granted as ONE exact, argument-less rule for the
# root-owned wrapper /usr/local/libexec/culvert-maint/compose-cli
# (packaging/culvert-maint/compose-cli, rendered and installed root:root
# 0755 by install.sh). The wrapper is the argument boundary: it admits
# exactly the argv the agent emits (a /backup/<name> path with the agent's
# filename rules, the 3 restore modes × 4 flag sets × {dryrun, commit} in
# canonical order, an --older-than duration of 1h..8760h, a --keep-last of
# 0..100, a 12..64 lowercase-hex container id, a
# <proxy_repo>@sha256:<64 hex> reference) and execs the same docker command
# these rules used to match. That is TIGHTER than the old wildcards: classic sudo's `*` matches
# any characters including spaces, so `/backup/*` also admitted traversal
# (`/backup/../x`) and extra appended flags.
#
# The remaining trailing `*` entries are the sole-trailing form both sudo
# implementations accept:
#   - image inspect * / manifest inspect --verbose *
#                — agent enforces the operator's image_allowlist regex
#                  (policy) AND a bounded argv-safety shape
#                  (^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9._/:@-]{0,511}$, no leading
#                  dash) before sudo is invoked. Both commands are
#                  read-only — they do not mutate the host or the stack.
#
# The static parity test
# (cmd/culvert-maint/internal/runner/parity_test.go) asserts the union
# of every Template.SudoersLines exactly matches the entries below: no
# missing, no dead surface.

# ─── env preservation (D1.6c — COMMAND-SCOPED, privilege hardening) ──
# sudo's default env_reset clears the child environment before exec'ing
# the allowed command. The agent builds the child env from scratch and
# passes per-call values via an overlay (it never inherits os.Environ),
# but those values still have to SURVIVE sudo to reach `docker compose`.
#
# The remaining var is preserved with a COMMAND-SCOPED `Defaults!<alias>`
# rather than a blanket `Defaults:culvert-maint env_keep` — least privilege:
# it crosses the sudo boundary ONLY for the command that consumes it, and is
# stripped for every other allowed command (ps, list-backups, image/manifest
# inspect, up, down, …):
#   - CULVERT_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE — reaches the cli container via `-e` for
#                                encrypted backup/restore. Preserved ONLY
#                                for the compose-cli wrapper, which itself
#                                UNSETS it for the subcommands that must not
#                                carry it (unencrypted `backup`, `cleanup`,
#                                `inspect-image`, `pull`, `tag`)
#                                and forwards it with `-e` only for
#                                `backup-encrypted` and `restore` — the same
#                                two docker commands the old per-command
#                                alias covered.
# The agent only ever sets it via an explicit overlay, and it is overlay-only
# on the agent side (never sourced from the agent's ambient env). The alias
# pattern below scopes env preservation only; it does NOT grant execution —
# a command must still match a NOPASSWD entry to run.
#
# P1.4 (D1.6c-pin-value-binding-plan.md): CULVERT_PROXY_IMAGE and its
# env_keep are GONE. The proxy image is no longer selected by an env var;
# it is bound at THIS boundary by the compose-cli wrapper's repo-literal
# `pull` + `tag` subcommands, so a direct sudo can no longer choose an
# arbitrary image.
Cmnd_Alias CULVERT_MAINT_PASSPHRASE_CMNDS = /usr/local/libexec/culvert-maint/compose-cli
Defaults!CULVERT_MAINT_PASSPHRASE_CMNDS env_keep += "CULVERT_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE"

# ─── compose.status (D1.6a) ─────────────────────────────────────────
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker compose -f {compose_path} ps --format json

# ─── compose.cli.backup.list (D1.6b) ────────────────────────────────
# Fixed argv, no variable part: allowlisted directly.
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker compose -f {compose_path} --profile cli run --rm cli --list-backups --backup-dir /backup

# ─── compose.cli.backup.{encrypted,unencrypted}, compose.cli.restore.{dryrun,
#     commit}, compose.cli.cleanup.{dryrun,commit} (D1.6b) ──────────────────
# ONE exact rule, no arguments, no wildcard: the root-owned wrapper is the
# argument boundary (see the header and packaging/culvert-maint/compose-cli).
# It execs, and ONLY execs, these docker commands:
#   backup-encrypted /backup/<name>
#     → … --profile cli run --rm -e CULVERT_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE cli --encrypt --backup /backup/<name>
#   backup /backup/<name>
#     → … --profile cli run --rm cli --backup /backup/<name>
#   restore /backup/<name> --mode <full|trust-root-only|state-only>
#           [--accept-dp-reenrollment] [--allow-counter-rollback] [--confirm]
#     → … --profile cli run --rm -e CULVERT_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE cli --restore /backup/<name> --mode <mode> [flags…]
#       (3 modes × 4 flag sets × {dryrun, commit} = the 24 combinations this
#       file used to enumerate; flags only in that canonical order)
#   cleanup --older-than <1h..8760h> --keep-last <0..100> [--confirm]
#     → … --profile cli run --rm cli --cleanup-restore-leftovers --older-than <d> --keep-last <n> [--confirm]
#   plus inspect-image / pull / tag for the upgrade path (see the last
#   section of this file).
# The compose file path is rendered INTO the root-owned wrapper by install.sh
# (it is not an argument), so the wrapper rule is path-bound like every
# direct `docker compose -f {compose_path}` rule here. State-changing variants
# (backup, restore --confirm, cleanup --confirm) acquire the agent's global
# maintenance lock before the runner invokes them.
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/local/libexec/culvert-maint/compose-cli

# ─── compose.down / compose.up (D1.6b — used by restore.commit) ─────
# The compose.up line has TWO forms: the plain single-`-f` recreate, and a
# two-`-f` variant that also merges an OPTIONAL override compose file so the
# maintenance-agent socket wiring survives an agent-driven recreate. install.sh
# renders the override path when compose_override_file is set, and DELETES the
# two-`-f` line entirely when it is not (so an un-configured host never carries
# an unresolved placeholder or extra surface — the deletion targets the sole
# line bearing that placeholder token, keeping this comment intact). Both files
# are fixed literals — never a wildcard.
#
# Each compose.up form also exists with a trailing --no-build. An appliance
# (compose_no_build = true) keeps ONLY the --no-build forms: its ClamAV sidecar
# is a scanned, baked image, so this host must never be able to rebuild a
# missing tag from source. Every other host keeps ONLY the plain forms. The
# installer deletes the other pair by its exact line ending.
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker compose -f {compose_path} down
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker compose -f {compose_path} up -d
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker compose -f {compose_path} -f {compose_override_path} up -d
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker compose -f {compose_path} up -d --no-build
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker compose -f {compose_path} -f {compose_override_path} up -d --no-build

# ─── image / manifest inspect (upgrade check, read-only) ────────────
# Single bounded `*` = the image_ref token, validated against
# image_allowlist + an argv-safety shape before sudo is invoked. These
# are global (non-compose) docker reads; they cannot mutate the stack.
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker image inspect *
culvert-maint ALL=(root) NOPASSWD: /usr/bin/docker manifest inspect --verbose *

# ─── container inspect + image pull/retag (upgrade apply / rollback) ──
# NO direct rule. These used to be enumerated here: one
# `docker inspect --format {{json\ .Image}} [0-9a-f]…` line per container-id
# length (12–64, 53 lines) and a `docker pull`/`docker tag`
# `<proxy_repo>@sha256\:[0-9a-f]…` pair with 64 hex classes. sudo-rs (Ubuntu
# 26.04's default sudo) parses those but compares arguments LITERALLY: a
# `[0-9a-f]` class is five characters there, and the backslash-escaped space
# splits `{{json .Image}}` into two arguments. They never matched, so the
# upgrade capture, verify and pull were refused on every sudo-rs host.
#
# They now run through the SAME compose-cli wrapper rule above, which
# validates the argv exactly as tightly as the enumerated rules did and execs:
#   inspect-image <12..64 lowercase hex>
#     → docker inspect --format '{{json .Image}}' <id>
#       (read-only; one id, nothing after it, `--format` fixed, so a second
#       `--format {{json .Config.Env}}` can never dump container secrets)
#   pull <proxy_repo>@sha256:<64 lowercase hex>
#     → docker pull <ref>
#   tag <proxy_repo>@sha256:<64 lowercase hex> culvert/proxy:pinned
#     → docker tag <ref> culvert/proxy:pinned
# The proxy image is still selected at the privilege boundary, not by an env
# var: proxy_repo is a literal rendered INTO the root-owned wrapper by
# install.sh (like the compose path), compared byte-for-byte, and the retag
# destination is the fixed culvert/proxy:pinned the compose file resolves. A
# compromised culvert-maint user can still not run a foreign image, pull by
# tag, or point the pinned tag anywhere else. pull/tag are state-changing
# (local image store). The wrapper unsets CULVERT_BACKUP_PASSPHRASE for all
# three, so the passphrase env_keep above never reaches these docker commands.
